# [Oriel 0.20.7 · October 30, 2026]($heading.id('oriel-0-20-7-october-30-2026'))

## [Fixed]($heading.id('fixed-0-20-7'))

- Windows installer: a silent install (`setup.exe /S`) on a machine without
  the WebView2 runtime waited forever on a message box it couldn't show; it
  now finishes. Every message box in the installer has a silent answer, and a
  test checks it.

# [Oriel 0.20.6 · October 30, 2026]($heading.id('oriel-0-20-6-october-30-2026'))

## [Fixed]($heading.id('fixed-0-20-6'))

- Video on Linux: an embedded player took the video's own size, spilling
  past its rectangle; the window's overlay now gives it exactly the page's
  rectangle (`get-child-position`).
- Video on Linux with NVIDIA's driver: GtkVideo's frames made GTK's Vulkan
  renderer run out of device memory and the app crash in the driver. Apps
  with the video module use the GL renderer there (`GSK_RENDERER=gl`,
  unless the user set one).

# [Oriel 0.20.5 · October 30, 2026]($heading.id('oriel-0-20-5-october-30-2026'))

## [Added]($heading.id('added-0-20-5'))

- Video: the player can be embedded in the page. `play({ url, rect })` puts
  it over that rectangle (CSS pixels), under the page's own bar and menus,
  with a fullscreen button; `setRect` follows the layout, `setFullscreen`
  covers the screen (window fullscreen on Linux; on Android the system bars
  hide and a landscape video turns the phone sideways), and Esc or Back come
  back. `video:fullscreen` (`{ url, fullscreen }`) reports the changes.
  `setVisible` hides the player while the page shows a menu or dialog over
  it, since native views draw above the page
  ([Video]($link.page('docs/video').ref('embedded'))).
- Android: `runtime.call` passes `f32` and `f64` arguments.

# [Oriel 0.20.4 · October 30, 2026]($heading.id('oriel-0-20-4-october-30-2026'))

## [Added]($heading.id('added-0-20-4'))

- The `oriel_updater` module: the self-updater's engine alone (standard
  library only), for an app's own command line tool to update itself from a
  signed manifest without linking GTK or a WebView
  ([Updater]($link.page('docs/updater').ref('cli-tools'))).

# [Oriel 0.20.3 · October 30, 2026]($heading.id('oriel-0-20-3-october-30-2026'))

## [Added]($heading.id('added-0-20-3'))

- `App.openPath(path, mime)`: opens a file on this device in an app for its
  MIME type, or a folder in the file manager. On Android the file goes through
  the chooser as a read-only `content://` URI (`OrielFileProvider`), so it
  works with any app, ChromeOS's included; a folder on shared storage opens in
  Files (DocumentsUI), anything else in Downloads. On Linux it is the default
  app for the type through GIO; elsewhere a `file://` URI to `openExternal`.

## [Fixed]($heading.id('fixed-0-20-3'))

- Android on ChromeOS: `openExternalAs` with a `127.0.0.1` URL no longer offers
  ChromeOS's own apps, which run outside the Android container and got
  "connection refused".

# [Oriel 0.20.2 · October 30, 2026]($heading.id('oriel-0-20-2-october-30-2026'))

## [Added]($heading.id('added-0-20-2'))

- Media server: apps can opt in to a listener on all IPv4 interfaces for
  capability URLs shared with devices on the LAN or a VPN. Numeric IPv4 Host
  headers are accepted only in this mode; the default remains loopback-only.

# [Oriel 0.20.1 · October 30, 2026]($heading.id('oriel-0-20-1-october-30-2026'))

## [Fixed]($heading.id('fixed-0-20-1'))

- CoreAudio: deduplicate AudioQueue and capture stream logic between macOS and
  iOS into `src/modules/audio_capture/apple.zig`.
- Android: deduplicate asynchronous request synchronization in `camera` and
  `dialog` modules using a reusable `AsyncRequest(T)` helper in
  `src/platform/android/async_request.zig`.
- Architecture: break circular imports between `src/core/` and concrete modules
  (`store`, `video`, `dialog`, `camera`, `tts`, `dictation`, `chat`), decoupling
  window geometry persistence and page IPC commands via runtime hooks.
- Native UI: decompose `paintCanvas` drawing dispatch in GTK native UI into
  specialized transform, path, and style command handlers, reducing cyclomatic
  complexity.
- Cross-platform native UI: deduplicate drag leave event dispatch and disabled
  state tree lookups across GTK, AppKit, Win32, Android, and UIKit.

# [Oriel 0.20.0 · October 28, 2026]($heading.id('oriel-0-20-0-october-28-2026'))

## [Added]($heading.id('added-0-20-0'))

- Video: `oriel.video`, a native player over the window for what the
  WebView's `<video>` won't play (camcorder MPEG-TS `.mts`/`.m2ts`,
  WMV/ASF, ProRes) or plays badly. It fills the window with the
  platform's controls, a title and a close button; the close button, Esc
  and Android's back show the page again. Zig: `oriel.video.play(window,
  .{ .url, .title })` and `close()`; the page: `oriel.video.play({ url,
  title })` (resolves once it shows), `oriel.video.close()` and
  `oriel.video.available`, with the events `video:ended`, `video:error`
  (`{ url, message }`) and `video:closed`. Linux: `GtkVideo` on a
  `GtkMediaFile` (GStreamer) with graphics offload, for `http(s)://` (the
  media server's URLs, with Range) and `file://`; web view windows' content
  is now a `GtkOverlay` holding the WebView while the module is built.
  Android: `MediaPlayer` on a `SurfaceView` with a `MediaController`
  (OrielVideo.kt); the app's media URLs (`https://app.localhost/media/...`)
  are read in-process from the media source or the root, as OrielMedia
  serves them. The `video` flag is on by default; Windows, macOS, iOS and
  `native_ui` builds don't have it (`available` is false). See
  docs/video.md.

## [Changed]($heading.id('changed-0-20-0'))

- Linux on NVIDIA (`/proc/driver/nvidia`, and now `/sys/module/nvidia` for
  the open kernel modules): Oriel sets `WEBKIT_DMABUF_RENDERER_FORCE_SHM=1`
  instead of `WEBKIT_DISABLE_DMABUF_RENDERER=1`, and only when neither is
  already set. Disabling the DMABUF renderer turned GPU compositing off: on
  an RTX 4070 (driver 615, WebKitGTK 2.54) a 1080p `<video>` presented
  almost no frames, at 73% of a core. Shared memory buffers avoid the GBM
  allocation that fails on some NVIDIA setups (a blank window, Wayland
  protocol error 71; WebKit bug 261874) and keep GPU rendering: 30 fps at
  61% of a core.

# [Oriel 0.19.7 · October 28, 2026]($heading.id('oriel-0-19-7-october-28-2026'))

## [Added]($heading.id('added-0-19-7'))

- Media sources: an app serves `<video>` and `<audio>` bytes from its own
  code (blocks fetched from another device, a file decrypted on the fly)
  instead of files below a folder, with HTTP Range, so playback starts at
  once and seeks work. A `Source` gives each path's size (`sizeFn`, null
  for 404) and copies bytes from an offset (`readFn`), thread-safe. On
  desktop, `media_server.Options.source` serves it on the loopback server
  in place of `root_dir`, with the same Host check, CORS, Range, HEAD and
  416, reading up to 256 KiB at a time. On Android,
  `media_scheme.setSource(source)` serves it at
  `https://app.localhost/media/` instead of the root until
  `clearSource()`: OrielMedia's stream reads through
  `NativeLib.mediaSize`/`mediaRead`, and the WebView's skip to a range's
  first byte only moves its position. Elsewhere `setSource` returns
  `error.Unsupported`.

# [Oriel 0.19.6 · October 27, 2026]($heading.id('oriel-0-19-6-october-27-2026'))

## [Added]($heading.id('added-0-19-6'))

- Android apps can keep running with no window in front, for work that
  isn't audio (a file server for the user's other devices, a sync engine):
  `.android.keep_running = "why"` declares a foreground service of type
  `specialUse` (the text is the subtype Google Play reviews), and
  `oriel.android.setKeepRunning(on, title, text, actions)` starts and stops
  it with an ongoing notification and its buttons. With
  `.window.hide_on_close`, swiping the app away from Recents leaves it
  running. `oriel.android.batteryUnrestricted()` and
  `oriel.android.openAppSettings()` help the user exempt it from battery
  optimization. The microphone service (`setForegroundService`) is
  unchanged.

# [Oriel 0.19.5 · October 26, 2026]($heading.id('oriel-0-19-5-october-26-2026'))

## [Added]($heading.id('added-0-19-5'))

- `llama.ChatTemplates.Options` takes the rest of llama.cpp's sampler:
  `top_p`, `top_k`, `min_p`, and the presence, frequency and repeat
  penalties (with `penalty_last_n`). Their defaults are llama.cpp's, so an
  answer samples as before unless a field is set. Agents on a small
  thinking model need them: near-greedy sampling without a penalty makes it
  repeat itself, calling the same tool turn after turn.

# [Oriel 0.19.4 · October 25, 2026]($heading.id('oriel-0-19-4-october-25-2026'))

## [Fixed]($heading.id('fixed-0-19-4'))

- Builds without `-Dtarget` or `-Dcpu` (CI's default) target the
  architecture's baseline CPU on every OS, not the build machine's
  (`resolveTarget`; Apple M1 on arm64 macOS). Release binaries built on a
  newer runner could use instructions older CPUs lack: the Linux system
  monitor crashed with SIGILL when checking for updates on a Zen 4 desktop.
  An explicit `-Dcpu`, `-Dcpu=native` included, still wins.
- The system monitor's update files are attached to the release again (its
  version had stayed at 0.19.0, failing that job since 0.19.1).

# [Oriel 0.19.3 · October 23, 2026]($heading.id('oriel-0-19-3-october-23-2026'))

## [Added]($heading.id('added-0-19-3'))

- Android: `audio_capture`'s "system" source (Android 10+): what other apps
  play, through AudioPlaybackCapture after MediaProjection's consent. With
  `.permissions.system_audio` the manifest declares
  `FOREGROUND_SERVICE_MEDIA_PROJECTION` and the foreground service gets the
  `mediaProjection` type. Apps that opt out of capture, and calls, stay
  silent.
- `tts`: `.engine = .system` reads with the platform's own voices
  (Android's TextToSpeech), nothing to download; `systemAvailable()` says
  whether the device has an engine. Same `tts:state` events and `stop()`.
- `media_scheme` on Android: local media at `https://app.localhost/media/`,
  served from the WebView's request interception with Range requests for
  seeking (no port, nothing read into memory). `oriel.media_scheme` is
  available without the HTTP media server, and `media_scheme.base_url` says
  where pages load the files from.

## [Fixed]($heading.id('fixed-0-19-3'))

- ChromeOS: showing a window that already has focus no longer minimizes it
  (moving the active task to the front is a shelf click there).

# [Oriel 0.19.2 · October 23, 2026]($heading.id('oriel-0-19-2-october-23-2026'))

## [Added]($heading.id('added-0-19-2'))

- `oriel.share` on Android. Receiving: `.share_target` declares `ACTION_SEND`
  intent filters on the main Activity (and `ACTION_SEND_MULTIPLE` with
  `multiple`), labelled in the share sheet with `label`; shared text, links
  (a text that is just an `http(s)` URL arrives as `url`, with `subject`) and
  files reach `onReceive` and the page's `share:received`, files as read-only
  handles (a pipe is copied into the cache first). Sending: `send` opens the
  system chooser with text, a URL and files (through the app's file
  provider); `done` hears the chosen app's package, or `completed: false`
  when the app is in front again without a choice.

## [Fixed]($heading.id('fixed-0-19-2'))

- Android: the file provider reports every shared file's MIME type, not only
  PNG and plain text.

# [Oriel 0.19.1 · October 23, 2026]($heading.id('oriel-0-19-1-october-23-2026'))

## [Changed]($heading.id('changed-0-19-1'))

- Publish the documentation site at `https://oriel.foreachdeveloper.com`
  through Cloudflare Pages, refreshing it after each release.
- Maintenance release to validate signed CLI updates from the replacement
  GitHub repository with the existing release-signing key.

# [Oriel 0.19.0 · October 20, 2026]($heading.id('oriel-0-19-0-october-20-2026'))

## [Added]($heading.id('added-0-19-0'))

- `oriel.llama.ChatTemplates`: a model's own Jinja chat template serves an
  OpenAI chat request the way llama-server does (llama.cpp's
  `common/chat.cpp`): messages with tool calls and results, tools,
  `response_format` and the thinking switch render in the model's native
  format; the answer samples with the template's lazy tool-call grammar and
  parses back into content, reasoning and tool calls, streamed as it grows.

## [Changed]($heading.id('changed-0-19-0'))

- `.llama` builds llama.cpp's `common` library (its CLI and download parts
  aside) instead of the JSON-schema converter alone.

# [Oriel 0.18.1 · October 19, 2026]($heading.id('oriel-0-18-1-october-19-2026'))

## [Added]($heading.id('added-0-18-1'))

- Tray: `.template_png` icons. A monochrome PNG (the mark in black on
  transparency) shown as an NSImage template on macOS, so the menu bar
  recolours it for light and dark mode itself; Linux and Windows draw the
  pixels like `.png`.

# [Oriel 0.18.0 · October 17, 2026]($heading.id('oriel-0-18-0-october-17-2026'))

The native renderer (`-Dnative_ui`) runs full apps that were written for a
web view: dialogs, disclosures, the session history, file and camera
inputs, date pickers, popovers, `Intl`, and the drawing such an app's CSS
asks for, drawn to match the web view build on Linux and Android.

## [Added]($heading.id('added-0-18-0'))

- `<dialog>`: `show()`, `showModal()` (the top layer over every
  `z-index`, with its `::backdrop`; `:modal`; the page outside inert, Tab
  kept inside), `close()` and `requestClose()`, `returnValue`, `cancel` on
  Escape and the platform's back, `close` after it, the focus back where it
  was, `method=dialog` forms.
- `<details>` and `<summary>`: closed, only the summary shows, with its
  disclosure triangle; a click, Enter or `open` toggles it and fires
  `toggle`.
- The Popover API: `popover`, `showPopover()`, `hidePopover()`,
  `togglePopover()`, `popovertarget` buttons, `:popover-open`,
  `beforetoggle` and `toggle`; auto popovers close on Escape, a press
  outside or another auto popover.
- The session history: `history.pushState`, `replaceState`, `state`,
  `back`, `forward`, `go`, `popstate` and `hashchange`; the platform's
  back closes a modal dialog, then walks the history.
- `<input type=file>`: the platform's file dialog (`accept` narrows it on
  the desktops), or with `capture` on a phone the camera; the pick is
  `files[0]` (read lazily, as a dropped file), then `input` and `change`;
  `cancel` when there's none. New page commands `nui:pick_file` and
  `nui:picked_file` (src/modules/dialog/page.zig).
- `<input type=date>`: the platform's date picker on every platform (a
  `GtkCalendar` popover, the Date and Time Picker control, an
  `NSDatePicker` popover, a `UIDatePicker`, `DatePickerDialog`), the value
  `YYYY-MM-DD`, the date shown in the user's format.
- `Intl`: `NumberFormat`, `DateTimeFormat`, `Collator`, `PluralRules` and
  the `toLocale*String` and `localeCompare` methods over them (English,
  Spanish, Portuguese, French, German and Italian data, matching ICU's
  output for the common options); `navigator.language` is the user's on
  Linux and Android.
- `new Image()` and `<img>`: `naturalWidth`/`naturalHeight` from the file's
  header, `complete`, `decode()`, `load` and `error`.
- `conic-gradient()` backgrounds (pie and donut charts); `background-image`
  gradients with `background-size`, `background-position` and
  `no-repeat`; `:empty` in style sheets; an `appearance: none` checkbox's
  `::before` and `::after` (a switch's knob); `form.elements`,
  `requestSubmit(submitter)` and `submit` events' `submitter`;
  `datalist.options`; `env()` (the safe-area insets are 0).
- `grid-column` places grid items (`1 / -1`, `span N`, a start line), as
  browsers auto-place them row by row; absolutely positioned children take
  no cell.
- `::placeholder` (and `::-webkit-input-placeholder`) colors a field's
  placeholder; without one it's the platform browser's (WebKit's
  `darkgray`, Chrome's `#757575`).
- Every CSS named color (`darkgray`, `crimson`, `rebeccapurple`…), not a
  dozen.
- `alert`, `confirm` and `prompt` answer as a browser with its dialogs
  suppressed (they would block the UI thread) and log a warning.
- Events the runtime fires are `isTrusted`; a promise rejected with no
  handler is logged.

## [Fixed]($heading.id('fixed-0-18-0'))

- The page starts after the app's `setup` on the desktops, as a web
  view's page does, so its first commands see what `setup` made (a tray).
- A block-level button centres its content only in its height (its
  `text-align` places the text); `justify-content` and gaps apply to flex
  and grid boxes only. The UA sheet centres a button's text.
- A select with `appearance: none` hides the platform's arrow when the
  page draws its own beside it; GTK's drop-down takes new options.
- Stylesheet and script links with a query (`styles.css?v=8`) load.
- SVG icons follow the page's CSS `fill`, `stroke` and `stroke-width` on
  the `<svg>`; an inline `::before`/`::after` text stays on its line.
- Android: fields inside a modal dialog show; a border on only some sides
  of a rotated box no longer draws hairlines on the others.
- A focused control that gets disabled or leaves the page loses the focus
  (the focus fixup rule), so its focus ring goes.
- `matches()`, `closest()` and `querySelector()` know `:focus`,
  `:focus-visible`, `:hover` and `:active`.
- A modal dialog with `margin: auto`, a set width and an auto height is
  centred, as tall as its content's room up to its `max-height`.
- Grid `fr` columns stay equal beside padded cells (a partial last row
  of `repeat(auto-fit, …)`), and an auto-fit grid's column count follows
  its laid-out width.
- `inline-flex` and `inline-grid` lay their children out as `flex` and
  `grid` do.
- Line heights are in WebKit's 1/64 px units, so text sits on the same
  pixel rows as the web view's; SVG elements have
  `getBoundingClientRect()`.
- GTK: text fields and selects take the page's `font-weight`, style and
  family, and place their text where WebKit does; `focus()` keeps a field's
  selection (Tab still selects its text); a field under a modal's backdrop
  shows its text, dimmed; a date shows its year in full; text fields use
  WebKitGTK's 1px padding and select widths leave room for the arrow only
  when it's drawn; `gtk-interface-color-scheme` (GTK 4.20, the desktop's
  light or dark) sets `prefers-color-scheme`, and a change of it applies
  at once.
- Android: a date shows its year in full, as Chrome does; a one-line
  field shows its text from the start until it's focused, and again once
  left; a field under a modal's backdrop stays drawn there, dimmed.
- A transparent box (`opacity: 0`, a faded-out toast) no longer hides the
  fields under it (Android cut a select's label to a sliver; GTK and
  Windows hid the field).
- `desktop-entry-release` / `desktop-entry` install what the packages put
  next to the executable (`libggml-cuda.so`, `libggml-vulkan.so`,
  `contents.files`), so an app installed that way finds its GPU backends.
- Phones: when the keyboard comes up, the focused field scrolls into what's
  left of the page, as Chrome and Safari bring it into view.

# [Oriel 0.17.3 · October 17, 2026]($heading.id('oriel-0-17-3-october-17-2026'))

## [Fixed]($heading.id('fixed-0-17-3'))

- Android: apps opened from the launcher icon now appear in Recents. The
  launcher activity passed its own exclusion from Recents on to the app's
  window; windows also clear it from tasks created by earlier versions.

# [Oriel 0.17.2 · October 16, 2026]($heading.id('oriel-0-17-2-october-16-2026'))

## [Fixed]($heading.id('fixed-0-17-2'))

- Windows: a failed allocation while reading the launch arguments freed
  them twice.
- Linux: the custom media scheme's empty responses (errors, 416) no
  longer read from a stack buffer after the handler returned.
- Linux tray: a menu child is no longer leaked when its parent's list
  runs out of memory.
- Windows: the custom media scheme answers 500 when it runs out of memory
  building its headers, instead of leaving the page waiting.
- System monitor (macOS): the process list no longer reads past its
  buffer when the buffer couldn't be allocated.

## [Changed]($heading.id('changed-0-17-2'))

- Assertions (Power of 10, rule 5) where outside data enters: IPC, the
  updater, the native DOM and the system monitor's samplers. The system
  monitor's release builds are ReleaseSafe.

# [Oriel 0.17.1 · October 15, 2026]($heading.id('oriel-0-17-1-october-15-2026'))

## [Added]($heading.id('added'))

- System monitor example: the version in the header and **Check for
  updates** next to Settings, with `oriel.updater`. Each release attaches a
  signed `system-monitor-latest.json`; a copy updates from its own entry
  (its renderer, an executable, the AppImage or the macOS `.app`), and a
  deb/rpm copy says a new version is out. The monitor now has Oriel's
  version instead of 0.1.0.

## [Changed]($heading.id('changed'))

- System monitor example: the Oriel renderer is the default
  (`-Drenderer=oriel`), and the release packages (AppImage, deb, rpm,
  Windows installer and zip, macOS `.app`) use it.

# [Oriel 0.17.0 · October 15, 2026]($heading.id('oriel-0-17-0-october-15-2026'))

Oriel tells voices apart: `oriel.speakers`, speaker embeddings and
offline diarization, the module behind GhostPen's meetings (who said
what, and named voices remembered across meetings).

## [Added]($heading.id('added-2'))

- `oriel.speakers` (`.speakers = true`, needs `.llama` or `.whisper`;
  Linux, Windows, macOS): speaker embeddings and offline diarization on
  voice-detect.cpp (MIT), compiled against the shared ggml build.
  `Encoder` loads a GGUF encoder from `catalog` (WeSpeaker ResNet34 by
  default, ECAPA-TDNN) and embeds 16 kHz speech; `diarize` embeds 3 s
  windows every 1.5 s over the speech regions, clusters them
  (average-linkage, cosine; or a known speaker count) and returns speaker
  turns with one centroid per speaker; `cluster` does the clustering on
  embeddings an app made itself; `match` finds a known voice for a
  centroid. Progress and cancellation for long recordings. On LibriSpeech
  voices WeSpeaker put one man's halves at cosine 0.73, two women at 0.22,
  a man and a woman at 0.08 (the ECAPA GGUF: 0.80, 0.70, 0.45, hence the
  default); on three one-minute conversations it found 3, 4 and 1 speakers
  for 3, 3 and 1. See [Speakers]($link.page('docs/speakers')).

# [Oriel 0.16.1 · October 15, 2026]($heading.id('oriel-0-16-1-october-15-2026'))

## [Added]($heading.id('added-3'))

- System monitor example: an **Applications** list, the default in all
  three renderers. Each desktop app shows its combined CPU, summed resident
  memory, threads and process count; **Show processes** opens its members
  as a parent/child tree, and **All processes** lists everything, as
  before. Apps are recognized from desktop entries (Linux), `.app` bundles
  (macOS) and window owners (Windows), with process ancestry. Its CI runs
  the model tests and builds the Oriel renderer on Windows and macOS.

## [Fixed]($heading.id('fixed'))

- `oriel.chat`: a Stop that comes after its reply ended no longer stops
  the next one. Tokens can reach the page in a burst after the model
  finished, and `cancel()` with no reply running stops the next to start,
  so a Stop pressed during the burst left the next message empty.
  `chat:token` events now carry their reply's id (`reply`), and
  `cancelReply(id)` stops that reply only while it runs; the showcase's
  Stop sends it. `cancel()` is unchanged.
- The showcase's chat UI test (iOS CI) no longer waits 5 minutes for a
  reply its own late Stop had cancelled: it stops only while the reply
  runs, and asks again after a reply with no tokens.
- System monitor (Oriel renderer): picking a sort key (CPU, Memory, ...)
  no longer flips the direction; only the direction button does, as in the
  web frontend.

# [Oriel 0.16.0 · October 15, 2026]($heading.id('oriel-0-16-0-october-15-2026'))

Oriel apps can advertise over Bluetooth LE: `oriel.bluetooth` sends a
beacon nearby devices see, from Zig or the page, on every platform.

## [Added]($heading.id('added-4'))

- `oriel.bluetooth`: Bluetooth LE advertising, a beacon nearby devices
  see (docs/bluetooth.md). The same API on every platform, with
  `capabilities()` saying what each stack can send; a field the device
  can't send is `error.Unsupported`, never dropped. Starts, failures and
  platform stops come later as the advertisement's state
  (`onAdvertiseState`, the page's `bluetooth:advertise` event). On when
  the app declares `.permissions.bluetooth` (or `-Dbluetooth`); the page
  gets `oriel.bluetooth.capabilities()`, `advertise(ad)` and `stop(id)`.
  - Android and ChromeOS: BluetoothLeAdvertiser, every field (tested on a
    Chromebook's ARC, one advertisement at a time there).
  - Linux: BlueZ over the system bus, every field (tested on a BlueZ
    adapter).
  - macOS and iOS: CBPeripheralManager, service UUIDs and the device name
    (Apple can't advertise service data); `advertise` while the
    permission is undecided shows the prompt (tested on macOS 15).
  - Windows: BluetoothLEAdvertisementPublisher, service UUIDs and service
    data, no device name (not yet tested on a Windows machine).

# [Oriel 0.15.1 · October 14, 2026]($heading.id('oriel-0-15-1-october-14-2026'))

## [Fixed]($heading.id('fixed-2'))

- Breakout (Oriel renderer): dragging a finger on the board moves the
  paddle on touch screens. The board now takes the drag when a finger goes
  down on it, as the page's `touchmove` `preventDefault` does, so Android
  no longer turns the drag into a scroll and cancels the pointer.

# [Oriel 0.15.0 · October 14, 2026]($heading.id('oriel-0-15-0-october-14-2026'))

A page drawn by the native renderer can now be ported to the Oriel renderer
and look the same: `.oriel` says everything the native renderer computes, a
page exports itself as views, and `zig build check-views` proves it.
HollerShare is the first app ported this way. GTK windows paint only what
changed.

## [Added]($heading.id('added-5'))

- `.oriel` views can say everything the native renderer computes from
  HTML and CSS, so an Oriel-renderer view can look exactly like the web
  page it came from: per-side borders and border colors, dashed and dotted
  borders, per-corner and elliptical radii, `calc()` lengths, gradients,
  transforms, outlines and focus halos, `position: relative` offsets and
  sticky, tables, scrollbar styling and color scheme, `align-content` and
  any flex direction, max-/fit-content text, keyboard hints for fields,
  SVG icons (`<icon>` with `<path>`s), and spans that are underlined,
  highlighted, boxed (inline `<code>`-style boxes) or links whose clicks
  reach the app as their own ids. `unset="..."` gives a prop no value. A
  test fails the build if a native prop is ever added that no attribute
  sets.
- Porting a page to the Oriel renderer: `ORIEL_NUI_EXPORT` and
  `ORIEL_NUI_EXPORT_SCRIPT` write a native-renderer page as `.oriel` views,
  its states merged as blocks, and `zig build check-views` proves each
  view compiles to the props the page had (docs/design/zig-ui-experiment.md,
  "Porting a page"). Character entities (`&amp;`, `&#10;`) in markup.
- `.oriel` blocks: `@when (state)` for the app's states
  (`View.setState`), combined with media conditions, up to 64 blocks per
  view, applied block by block instead of one compiled variant per
  combination; `@when (focus)` and `@when (hover)` looks for the focused
  and hovered nodes, which also style native fields' focus. Spans take
  `class`.
- `.oriel` views update in place: `View.update(tree, base, bindings)` edits
  only the bound texts, tooltips and labels that changed, keeping the
  view's native controls, scroll positions and media styles. Typed
  property updates go straight to the tree (and Android's mirror) without
  JSON. `zig build bench-ui-updates -Dzig_ui=true` measures both.
- Zig-renderer apps: `Engine.setColorScheme` pins dark or light;
  `Driver.drag` takes drags and drops (dropped files' paths from
  `Engine.drops`); events the app emits reach `Driver.event` as "message"
  on every platform, as they did on Windows and Android.

## [Improved]($heading.id('improved'))

- GTK: windows paint only what changed since the last frame (tiles and
  patches GTK composites), and canvases are drawn at the display's real
  fractional scale instead of being resampled every frame. An idle system
  monitor took 3.7-7.4% of a core, now 1.5%. `ORIEL_NUI_FULL_REDRAW=1`
  paints everything, to compare.

## [Fixed]($heading.id('fixed-3'))

- Typed property updates keep the props a backend callback still reads
  until it returns.
- System monitor (Oriel renderer): the process actions' copy submenu shows,
  and copying or ending a process says whether it worked.
- A view mounted where another one was starts from its own look (it showed
  the old view's media blocks as already applied).
- Linux: a `-Dnative_ui` build no longer links a WebKitGTK call for
  transparent windows.
- iOS (native and Oriel renderers): the status bar and home indicator areas
  show the page's background, as CSS spreads the root's over the canvas,
  instead of the system's white; the status bar's text follows its
  lightness.
- System monitor (Oriel renderer): no "0.0 GHz" on Apple Silicon, which
  doesn't tell its CPU frequency; the frequency is hidden, as in the web
  frontend, and the per-core chart drops its "0.0G".
- Render bench: memory readings on macOS and iOS (resident size and
  physical footprint, the app's process), instead of 0 MB.
- Breakout: `zig build test-game` passes with any renderer (the WebView
  build left out the canvas the physics rules use).
- iOS simulator builds no longer print codesign's "replacing existing
  signature" as a failed command.

# [Oriel 0.14.1 · October 13, 2026]($heading.id('oriel-0-14-1-october-13-2026'))

## [Fixed]($heading.id('fixed-4'))

- The system monitor's packages (.deb, .rpm, .AppImage, .dmg, Windows
  installer and zip) are back on the release: 0.14.0's moved the example's
  sources to `src/` and its package builds still looked for the old files.

# [Oriel 0.14.0 · October 13, 2026]($heading.id('oriel-0-14-0-october-13-2026'))

The Oriel renderer grows up enough to run a real interface: `.oriel` views
take a style sheet with media blocks (a phone layout and a desktop one from
the same view), styled text runs, and `oriel fmt` keeps them tidy. The
Breakout example now runs in all three renderers and fits a phone held
upright.

## [Added]($heading.id('added-6'))

- `.oriel` text with `<span>`s: runs in their own color, weight, size,
  font or monospace, with `{binding}` text, spaces collapsed as HTML does
  ("Score **0**" as one text, the way the native renderer flattens it).
- `.oriel` colors as `rgba(r, g, b, a)`, `clickable` and
  `box-sizing="content-box"` attributes; `visible` is inherited as CSS's
  visibility (a hidden panel hides the platform's widgets in it), and
  `ui.setVisible` shows or hides a part and what's in it.
- `.oriel` views take a `<style>` sheet: `$tokens`, named styles applied with
  `class`, and up to four `@media (max-width: …)` / `(prefers-color-scheme:
  …)` blocks, switched as the window's width or theme changes;
  `width="auto"` lets a media block undo a size.
- `oriel fmt` formats `.oriel` views (`--check` for CI, `--stdin` for
  editors); apps get it as `zig build fmt-views`.
- `ORIEL_NUI_DUMP=props` dumps each node's props, and `ORIEL_NUI_DUMP_AT`
  dumps once after that many seconds, in both renderers.
- The Breakout example runs in the Oriel renderer (`-Drenderer=oriel`),
  looking the same as the native renderer's page.

## [Changed]($heading.id('changed-2'))

- The Breakout example picks its renderer with
  `-Drenderer=webview|native|oriel`; its `-Dnative_ui` option is gone.

## [Fixed]($heading.id('fixed-5'))

- `.oriel` `display="none"` hides what's in it on every backend: Android
  still drew a hidden text, laid out at width 0 (a letter a line).
- Breakout: a narrow window keeps the header on one line (Settings no
  longer drops below the score as it grows) and opens the settings panel
  across the board, its sliders full width; the Oriel renderer's view
  carries the page's `@media (max-width: 520px)` in its `<style>`.
- Native renderer (Linux): text fields and buttons partly scrolled out or
  drawn over show the part in view, instead of disappearing whole.
- Native renderer (Linux): text with `letter-spacing` broke before its last
  letter ("Breako-ut"): its width now includes the spacing after the last
  letter, as CSS's does.

# [Oriel 0.13.0 · October 13, 2026]($heading.id('oriel-0-13-0-october-13-2026'))


Oriel's two native front ends now stand on shared layers: one engine core
and one set of input rules under the native renderer (your HTML, CSS and
JavaScript) and the experimental Oriel renderer (`.oriel` views run from
Zig). A fix or feature in a shared layer reaches both. The new
[Architecture]($link.page('docs/architecture')) page maps the layers and where new
behavior belongs. **Breaking** only for the example apps' build flags (see
Changed).

## [Added]($heading.id('added-7'))

- **Oriel renderer (experimental, `-Dzig_ui`)**: `.oriel` markup compiled at
  build time into native views driven from Zig (`oriel.ui`), without
  QuickJS or the DOM. Elements: `row`, `column`, `view`, `text`, `button`
  (drawn), `control` (the platform's button), `input`, `textarea`,
  `select`/`option`, `checkbox`, `radio`, `slider`, `image`, `canvas`;
  attributes for layout, drawing, text, accessibility (`role`, `label`,
  `description`, `level`, `alt`) and `tooltip`, with `{binding}` text,
  labels and tooltips. Drawn elements get accessibility entries; Tab,
  focus rings, Enter/Space, checkboxes, radios and key scrolling behave as
  the platform's WebView. Linux; builds for Android. Not yet for apps.
- **Tooltips** on native-renderer nodes (`Props.tip`), shown by GTK.
- **Docs:** an Architecture page; diagrams are images rendered ahead of
  time (`tools/docs/diagrams.mjs`), readable on a phone, and a tap opens
  one full size.

## [Changed]($heading.id('changed-3'))

- **One engine core** (`src/native_ui/engine_core.zig`) for both front
  ends: frame callbacks, painting at most once per display frame (the Zig
  engine now coalesces events as the JavaScript one did), a timer when a
  backend has no frame clock, scrolling.
- **One set of input rules** (`src/native_ui/interaction.zig`), moved out of
  the runtime's JavaScript: focus rings by element and platform, Tab's
  order (macOS without Full Keyboard Access, iOS, radio groups, positive
  tabindex), what a click focuses, Enter/Space activation, key scroll steps.
  A key scroll's glide runs in Zig on display frames, with no JavaScript
  each frame. Pages see no API change.
- **Example apps** (`render-bench`, `system-monitor`) pick their renderer
  with `-Drenderer=webview|native|oriel`; their `-Dnative_ui` option is
  gone (`zig build -Drenderer=native`).
- The runtime's Node tests take the input rules from the engine, compiled
  to WebAssembly (`zig build interaction-wasm`, run by `npm test`).

## [Fixed]($heading.id('fixed-6'))

- Native renderer: a frame callback (`oriel.canvas`'s `onFrame`) that
  cancelled itself during a frame could crash or skip the next callback.
- 32-bit Android (armv7, x86) builds again.
- Native renderer: Tab with thousands of radios or a huge tabindex no
  longer stalls or stops working; host functions report a page's thrown
  conversions instead of ignoring them.

# [Oriel 0.12.1 · October 13, 2026]($heading.id('oriel-0-12-1-october-13-2026'))

## [Fixed]($heading.id('fixed-7'))

- Linux/Wayland: an overlay window (a layer surface) never grabs the
  keyboard any more. One with `focus_on_show` is focused when shown and lets go
  when the user clicks another app, as on every other platform (it held the
  keyboard until hidden). One with `focus_on_show = false` no longer takes the
  keyboard when it appears (Hyprland focused it), and one click still focuses
  it.

# [Oriel 0.12.0 · October 12, 2026]($heading.id('oriel-0-12-0-october-12-2026'))

Oriel 0.12.0 keeps one implementation and one API per feature: every second
way of doing something is gone, the generic, multiplatform form stays. Apps
declare everything once, in `build.zig`, and `App.Config` takes it from there.
The docs are rewritten as one page per feature, each with where it works, a
quick start, how it works (with a diagram), examples and the full reference.
**Breaking** throughout: each feature page has a "Migrating from 0.11"
section, and the table below lists every change.

## [Changed]($heading.id('changed-4'))

- **`App.Config` takes `.app = oriel_app.build` and `.window`.** Everything
  `build.zig` declares (id, embedded assets, dev server, icon, URL schemes,
  permissions, isolation) comes from `app.build`; the main window is a
  `WindowOptions` (`.window = .{ .title, .width, .height, ... }`, default
  800×600). `oriel_app` exports `build` and `update_public_key` only.
  `oriel.main` is the one entry point.
- **Android platform hooks are declared like the Apple ones:**
  `.android.hooks = &.{ .{ .class, .sources, .dependencies } }`, implementing
  `OrielAndroidHooks` (Kotlin and Java). The generated registry is
  `OrielHooksRegistry.kt`; runtime files a newer Oriel no longer ships are
  deleted from the app's Gradle project on the next build.
- **Events are typed:** `App.events(Events).emit` and `.emitTo(label, ...)`
  (any thread) are the app's way; the untyped `App.emit`/`emitTo` and
  `Window.emit` are Oriel-internal.
- **`oriel.paths` is the one place for app directories:** `appData`,
  `appCache`, `appConfig` and `appExternal` (Android's external files dir).
- **One `window.oriel` for both renderers** (`src/core/bridge_api.js`), one
  bridge script for every web view (`src/core/bridge_js.zig`), one dispatch
  for native pages' commands (`src/core/native_commands.zig`).
- **Shutdown cleanup runs on `oriel.lifecycle`'s `terminating`**, sent by
  every shell before its windows go (Oriel's own modules included).
- **Deep links are delivered through the same queue as notification clicks,
  shares and lifecycle events**: links that arrive before `onOpen` is set are
  handed to it once it is.
- **chat:** a reply holds its own lock: `status`, `delete` and `compare` no
  longer wait for it, and `Status.generating` is true while one runs;
  `chat.deinit` frees the model at exit; a `cancel()` sent before the reply
  starts stops it.
- **Catalogs** in chat, dictation and tts use `findModel(name) ?*const Model`
  and `modelPresent(m)`.
- Module namespaces show only their documented API: backends, shells' entry
  points and test hooks moved out of reach.

## [Removed (each with its one replacement)]($heading.id('removed'))

| Removed | Use |
|---|---|
| `App.Config.id`, `.title`, `.width`… `.placement`, `.show_main_window`, `.on_close`, `.icon`, `.deep_link_schemes`, `.permissions`, `.assets`, `.dev`, `.security.isolation` | `.app = app.build`, `.window = .{ ... }` (`.visible`, `.hide_on_close`) |
| `App.Config.on_session_end` | `oriel.lifecycle` `.terminating` |
| `App.run`, `App.setProcessArgs` | `oriel.main` |
| `App.showWindow`/`hideWindow`/`toggleWindow`, `App.closeWindow`/`postCloseWindow` | `App.getWindow(label)` and the `Window`'s methods; `Window.close()` |
| `App.setMenu` | `oriel.menu.set` |
| `App.gtk_app`, `App.main_window` and other App internals | `oriel.hooks` (native handles) |
| `.android.extensions`, `OrielAndroidExtension(Context)` | `.android.hooks` entries, `OrielAndroidHooks(Context)` |
| `oriel.ios.onSystemEvent`; Android `trim-memory` in `onSystemEvent` | `oriel.lifecycle` |
| `oriel.android.preconnect` | nothing: the app's `io` resolves through bionic |
| `PackageOptions.icon`, `PackageOptions.url_schemes` | `AppOptions.icon`, `AppOptions.url_schemes` |
| `oriel.store.configDir`/`dataDir`/`cacheDir`, `oriel.android.paths` | `oriel.paths` |
| `oriel.network.info`, `onChange` (never implemented) | — |
| `oriel.deep_link.validateUrl` | `validate` |
| `oriel.share.read` | `oriel.share.open` |
| `oriel.input.keyComboX11`, `typeTextX11` | `keyCombo`, `typeText` |
| `oriel.updater.init`/`deinit`, `downloadWithOptions`, `verifyManifestWithOptions`, test hooks | `oriel.main` does init; `download(io, gpa, u, .{ ... })` |
| `audio_play` one-shot `start`/`stop`/`finished` | `audio_play.Stream` |
| `dictation.sources` | `oriel.audio_capture.listSources` |
| `chat`/`dictation` `find`/`present` | `findModel`/`modelPresent` |
| `system_ai.download`/`generate` `io` parameter | — |
| `oriel.canvas.openIn`/`onFrameIn`/`engineOf` | `open`, `onFrame` |
| `window.oriel.native` | `oriel.features.nativeUi` |
| `-Dnative_dom=false` (linkedom in apps) | the native DOM |
| `oriel:window:open` `min_width` spelling | `minWidth` |
| IPC `notification:ready`, `deep_link:ready` | `events:ready` (the bridge's `listen()`) |
| `oriel pake`, `oriel setup zig`, `oriel setup webview2`, `oriel wrap --tray` | `oriel wrap`, `oriel zig install`, `oriel webview2` (`oriel setup all` installs both); the tray is the default |

## [Fixed]($heading.id('fixed-8'))

- Windows and Android: `const { openExternal } = oriel` threw (the WebView2
  bridge used `this.invoke`).
- `oriel.drop.path` works on macOS, iOS and Android (only Linux and Windows
  routed it) and resolves a string or null.
- `fs_watch` on Linux and Android: `poll` into `oriel.fs_watch.Event` didn't
  compile.
- `oriel deep-link add` writes `AppOptions.url_schemes`; `oriel permission`
  accepts `bluetooth` and `local_network`; `--help` shows the full usage of
  `permission`, `deep-link` and `desktop-entry`.
- The generated TypeScript has `WindowHandle.startDragging()`, the
  `window:created`/`window:closed` events and `oriel.drop`.

## [Docs]($heading.id('docs'))

- One page per feature (`site/content/docs/*.smd`, the website), with a
  Mermaid diagram on each; `docs/*.md` is generated from them
  (`tools/docs/gen-md.mjs`, checked in CI). Design records are in
  `docs/design/`, contributor notes in `docs/internal/` (the page template is
  `docs/internal/writing-docs.md`).
- The examples no longer commit Oriel's generated Kotlin runtime.

# [Oriel 0.11.0 · October 12, 2026]($heading.id('oriel-0-11-0-october-12-2026'))

Oriel 0.11.0 brings platform hooks to every platform: an app's own Kotlin,
Objective-C, Swift or Zig code reacts to, or takes over, the platform's app,
window and web view events, as Android's hooks already could. It also adds
Objective-C and Swift app extensions on macOS, Swift sources that link any
Apple framework they import, an iOS hook for background downloads, and
simulator builds that carry their entitlements like Xcode's. **Breaking:**
`.ios.resources` takes `.{ .from, .to }` entries. Validated on Linux,
Android (Poco X3), macOS 15.2, the iOS 18.4 simulator and CI's Xcode 26;
the Windows hooks compile but haven't run on Windows yet.

## [Added]($heading.id('added-8'))

- **Platform hooks in Zig: `oriel.hooks`** (Linux, macOS, Windows). The app's
  Zig code sees each window and web view Oriel creates, with its native
  handle (GtkWindow* / WebKitWebView*, NSWindow* / WKWebView*, HWND /
  ICoreWebView2* and its controller): `oriel.hooks.subscribe(.{
  .window_created, .webview_created, .webview_destroyed, .window_destroyed })`,
  up to 16 subscriptions, on the main thread. `webview_created` runs after
  Oriel installs its bridge, before the first navigation. `oriel.hooks.available`
  is false on Android and iOS, which hook natively. The showcase logs its
  windows and turns on a WebKitGTK setting through the handle. New page:
  [Platform hooks]($link.page('docs/platform-hooks')).
- **iOS and macOS platform hooks: `.ios.hooks`, `.macos.hooks`.** Objective-C
  or Swift classes conforming to `@protocol OrielPlatformHooks`
  (`oriel_hooks.h`) take part in Oriel's app and scene delegates, windows and
  WebKit delegates: `registered:` (with an `OrielHooksContext`:
  `presentingViewController` on iOS, `keyWindow` on macOS),
  `webViewConfiguration:window:`, `webViewCreated:window:` (before the first
  navigation), `webViewDestroyed:window:`, the push token and its failure,
  iOS scene events and `applicationDidFinishLaunching:options:`, macOS
  `applicationDidFinishLaunching:`, `windowCreated:label:` and
  `windowWillClose:label:`. Interceptions return `YES` to own the request
  (first `YES` wins, declaration order): navigation policy, media capture,
  `openURL:` (before `oriel.deep_link`), `continueUserActivity:` (universal
  links, Handoff; Oriel now implements `scene:continueUserActivity:` and
  `application:continueUserActivity:restorationHandler:`) and, on macOS,
  `applicationShouldHandleReopen:hasVisibleWindows:`; on iOS,
  `handleEventsForBackgroundURLSession:completionHandler:` (Oriel's app
  delegate now forwards background URLSession events, so downloads can
  continue while the app is suspended). Up to 16 hooks per
  platform, constructed once at launch before the first scene or window; a
  class that is also an app extension gets one instance for both. The
  showcase's hooks log what they see and own example.com links (an in-app
  Safari sheet on iOS). [Platform hooks]($link.page('docs/platform-hooks').ref('apple-protocol'))
- **App extensions in Objective-C or Swift on macOS:**
  `.extensions[].macos = .{ .objc = .{ .class, .sources, .swift_sources,
  .frameworks, .weak_frameworks } }` runs the iOS protocol and runtime
  (`OrielAppExtension`) on macOS; `.macos = .{ .zig = ... }` keeps the Zig
  backend. Either overrides `.desktop`. Native macOS sources build against
  the macOS SDK (`-Dmacos_sdk`, `$SDKROOT` or `xcrun`, so a Mac); Swift on
  macOS raises the minimum to macOS 15 (an explicit lower target is a build
  error), as Swift does to iOS 18. The showcase's `-Dobjc_echo` builds
  `showcase.echo` in Objective-C on macOS. [App extensions]($link.page('docs/extensions'))

## [Changed]($heading.id('changed-5'))

- **Breaking: `.ios.resources` takes named entries,** like Android's
  `.assets`: `.{ .from = <LazyPath>, .to = "models/yoloe.mlmodelc" }`, placed
  at `to` inside the bundle. `from` may now be a dependency's path
  (`dep.path(...)`, e.g. a hash-pinned, lazy model archive in build.zig.zon),
  not only a source path; a build step's output is refused. `to` is checked
  like Android asset paths (relative, no `..`).
- **Swift sources link the Swift overlays and frameworks they import**
  (iOS and macOS, extensions and hooks): Zig's linker ignores swiftc's
  autolink entries, so `import CoreML`, `Metal`, `Accelerate`, `simd`,
  `os`, … failed with undefined `__swift_FORCE_LOAD_$_swift...` symbols.
  Oriel now links the frameworks a Swift source imports and every
  `libswift*.tbd` of the SDK with `dead_strip_dylibs` (only what is used is
  loaded); every framework and library linked otherwise is marked needed,
  since Oriel looks classes up by name. The showcase's Swift hook imports
  CoreML, CoreGraphics and Metal.
- Swift sources (extensions' and hooks') use `oriel_swift.h` as their
  bridging header: both `oriel_extension.h` and `oriel_hooks.h`, so one
  Swift class can conform to both protocols.

- **`.android.hooks`** is the name of Android's Kotlin/Java hook classes
  (formerly `.android.extensions`), and `OrielAndroidHooks` /
  `OrielAndroidHooksContext` are Kotlin typealiases of `OrielAndroidExtension`
  / `OrielAndroidExtensionContext`. The Android page is now
  [Android platform hooks]($link.page('docs/platform-hooks')) (same URL). The
  showcase registers a hook class with `.android.hooks`.

## [Deprecated]($heading.id('deprecated'))

- `.android.extensions`: still registered, after the `.android.hooks`
  classes; a class named in both is registered once.

## [Fixed]($heading.id('fixed-9'))

- **iOS simulator builds carry entitlements like Xcode's.** The executable
  embeds `application-identifier` (`<team>.<bundle id>`, or `.<bundle id>`
  without `.ios.team_id`), `get-task-allow` and the keychain access group
  in its `__TEXT,__entitlements` and `__TEXT,__ents_der` sections, and on a
  Mac the bundle is signed ad hoc as a whole. Before, the simulator's
  daemons saw no bundle ID: nsurlsessiond refused background URLSessions
  ("does not have a bundle ID") and the keychain failed. New option:
  `.ios.team_id`.
- Docs: the site's "Intercept file selection" still told apps to build their
  own camera capture route; since 0.10 `oriel.camera` handles
  `<input type="file" accept="image/*" capture>` (Oriel's capture provider,
  the photo picker, HEIC/AVIF to JPEG), and a custom content provider is only
  for apps that need their own capture flow.

# [Oriel 0.10.0 · October 12, 2026]($heading.id('oriel-0-10-0-october-12-2026'))

Oriel 0.10.0 adds what porting GhostPantry to iOS showed was missing, so
apps no longer write platform code around Oriel: one lifecycle API on every
platform, module flags that resolve per target at compile time, app
extensions (an app's own Kotlin, Objective-C, Swift or Zig, called with one
API), photo capture, the device's own language model (Gemini Nano on
Android, Apple Foundation Models on iOS 26) and app directories. See
[App extensions]($link.page('docs/extensions')). **Breaking:** Android's stub
modules are gone; guard calls to desktop-only modules with
`if (oriel.<module>.available)`.

## [Added]($heading.id('added-9'))

- `oriel.lifecycle`: the app's lifecycle with one API on every platform
  (core, always on). `subscribe(handler)` (up to 16, called on the main
  thread in order) gets `background` / `foreground` (Android: the last
  Activity stopped / one started again; iOS: didEnterBackground /
  willEnterForeground), `memory_pressure` (`moderate` or `critical`:
  Android's onTrimMemory, iOS memory warnings, macOS's memory-pressure
  source, Windows' low-memory notification, Linux's GMemoryMonitor),
  `suspend_` / `resume_` (macOS NSWorkspace sleep and wake, Windows
  WM_POWERBROADCAST, logind PrepareForSleep) and `terminating` (quit,
  SIGTERM, iOS applicationWillTerminate, Windows WM_ENDSESSION). The page
  gets them with `oriel.lifecycle.on((e) => …)` as `{ type, level }`,
  queued until it listens; the TypeScript bindings declare
  `LifecycleEvent` and `LifecycleApi`.
- `oriel.chat`, `oriel.dictation` and `oriel.tts` unload their models on
  critical memory pressure by themselves; `unload_on` changes when
  (`.{ .background = true }`, or `.{ .memory_pressure = null }` for never).
- Every module namespace has a comptime `available` (see Changed, module
  flags). `window.oriel.features` in every page (and the native renderer)
  has the build's resolved flags in camelCase (`{ tray: false, fsWatch:
  true, ... }`), typed as `Features` in the generated `oriel.ts`.
  `zig build --verbose` prints one line listing the flags resolved to
  unavailable for the target.
- App extensions (`oriel.extensions`, docs/extensions.md): an app's own
  native code, declared by name in `AppOptions.extensions` with an
  implementation per platform: Kotlin (`.android`: `OrielAppExtension`,
  `OrielCall`, `OrielExtensionError`; sources, Maven dependencies, assets,
  generated R8 keep rules), Objective-C (`.ios`: the `OrielAppExtension`
  protocol in `oriel_extension.h`, compiled with `-fobjc-arc`; frameworks,
  bundle resources), opt-in Swift (`.ios.swift_sources`, with `swiftc` from
  Xcode or the Linux toolchain plus xtool's SDK; it raises the app's
  minimum to iOS 18) and Zig (`.desktop`, or `.linux`/`.macos`/`.windows`).
  `oriel.extensions.get("name")` is checked at build time and
  `available()` is comptime per target; `call(arena, method, input,
  .{ .timeout_ms, .cancel, .diag })` runs on the extension's own workers
  (`.concurrency`), with capped inputs and replies (`.max_bytes`, 32 MiB),
  a bounded queue, cancellation and timeouts, and is refused on the main
  thread. Native code emits events (`onEvent`; the page's
  `extension:<name>`) and gets `oriel.lifecycle`.
- The page reaches extensions only through Zig: an app command, or
  `oriel.extensions.expose(name, .{ .methods, .filter })` and
  `oriel.extensions.call(name, method, payload)` / `.on(name, cb)` in every
  bridge (TypeScript `ExtensionsApi`). The showcase's `showcase.echo` runs
  in Zig, Kotlin and Objective-C, and `-Dswift_example` adds
  `showcase.swift` on iOS; the `extension` UI test calls both where built.
- Oriel's own app extensions: modules can be built on the extension
  primitive (`oriel.system_ai` is the first: Kotlin and Swift backends
  shipped in Oriel, merged before the app's). Names starting with `oriel.`
  are reserved: an app can't declare or expose them. `.ios.weak_frameworks`
  weak-links frameworks newer than the app's minimum, only when the SDK has
  them.
- `oriel.camera` (`-Dcamera`, and on when the app declares
  `.permissions.camera`; docs/modules.md, "Camera"):
  `capturePhoto(io, arena, .{ .source = .camera | .library, .max_side,
  .format, .quality })` returns the photo upright, scaled and encoded
  (`bytes`, `width`, `height`, `mime`), with `error.Cancelled`,
  `Unsupported`, `PermissionDenied` and `TooLarge`. Android: the camera app
  into Oriel's capture provider and the system photo picker, scaled in
  Kotlin. iOS: `UIImagePickerController` and `PHPickerViewController`.
  Desktop: `.library` through the file dialog (filtered to images) and
  zigimg, with a JPEG's EXIF orientation applied; `.camera` Unsupported.
  The page: `oriel.camera.capturePhoto({ source, maxSide })` →
  `{ dataUrl, width, height, mime }` (TypeScript `CameraApi`).
- Android, with the camera module on: `<input type="file"
  accept="image/*">` opens the camera app (`capture`; CAMERA asked first)
  or the system photo picker (`ACTION_PICK_IMAGES` on 13+, several with
  `multiple`), and HEIC/AVIF picks are transcoded to JPEG
  (`AppOptions.camera.transcode`, default true). The generated
  `<app id>.oriel.capture` provider serves only Oriel's own captures.
  Apps no longer need their own camera extension; extensions still see
  the chooser first. Declaring `.permissions.camera` also declares
  `android.hardware.camera` as not required.
- `oriel.dialog.openFile`'s `.filter = .{ .name, .extensions }` (desktop).
- `oriel.system_ai` (`-Dsystem_ai`, off by default; Android and iOS): the
  device's own language model. `status()` (available, downloadable,
  downloading, unavailable, with a reason), `capabilities()`,
  `download(io, on_progress)`, `generate(io, arena, .{ .system, .prompt,
  .images, .temperature, .max_tokens, .schema }, on_token)`, `cancel()` and
  `lastError()`; one generate or download at a time, from a worker; caps of
  64 KiB per text, 4 images of 8 MiB and a 1 MiB reply; timeouts of 20 s,
  3 min and 10 min. Android: Gemini Nano through ML Kit's GenAI Prompt API
  (`genai-prompt` 1.0.0-beta4, added to Gradle only with the flag), text
  and one image, streaming, download progress. iOS: Apple Foundation
  Models (iOS 26+ with Apple Intelligence; text only), weak-linked and
  compiled only when the SDK has it; Oriel's Swift raises the app's minimum
  to iOS 18. The page: `oriel.systemAi.status/capabilities/download/
  generate/cancel` with `system_ai:token` and `system_ai:download` events
  (TypeScript `SystemAiApi`), in the native renderer too; its promises
  reject with a lowercase code and the platform's message, like `status()`
  (`"unavailable: …"`, `"busy"`). `oriel.features.systemAi`. Linux,
  Windows and macOS report `available = false`.
- Android builds with `-Dsystem_ai` stop with the lines to change when the
  Gradle project's Kotlin plugin is older than 2.3 (projects generated
  before 0.10 have 2.0.21; ML Kit needs 2.3). See docs/android.md,
  "Upgrading an existing project".
- Showcase: a Camera card on the Files tab and a `camera` UI test; a System
  AI card (`-Dsystem_ai`).
- `oriel.paths` (core): `appData(gpa, .{ .name })` and `appCache(gpa,
  .{ .name })`, the app's private data and cache directories on every
  platform, created if missing. Android: the files and cache dirs
  themselves (no subfolder: the sandbox is the app's); elsewhere the same
  folders as `oriel.store.dataDir` / `cacheDir` (XDG, `Library/Application
  Support` and `Library/Caches`, `%LOCALAPPDATA%`). Apps no longer reach
  for `oriel.android.paths.filesDir()` behind an Android check
  (docs/modules.md, "App directories").

## [Changed]($heading.id('changed-6'))

- Module flags are said once, for every target. An app's `.tray = true` in
  `b.dependency("oriel", ...)` means "where it exists": on a target without
  the module it resolves to off instead of failing the build (iOS) or
  building a stub (Android). Guard calls with `if (oriel.tray.available)`,
  and the code under it isn't built where the module is missing. A use
  outside the guard is a build error naming the module and the guard; so is
  one of a module the app left off. `-Dtray=true` on Oriel's own command
  line for a target without a tray is still a build error. Apps no longer
  need per-target flags like `.tray = !is_android`. See docs/modules.md,
  "Availability".
- **Breaking:** Android's stub modules are gone: `tray`, `menu` and `input`
  no longer build there with calls that fail with `error.Unsupported`. An
  Android build that calls them must guard the calls with
  `if (oriel.tray.available)` (or `oriel.menu`, `oriel.input`), and code
  that branched on `oriel.options.tray` can use `oriel.tray.available`.
- **Breaking:** `-Dlayer_shell` on Oriel's own command line is a build
  error outside desktop Linux (it was ignored on Windows and macOS); as an
  app flag it resolves to off there.
- Android: `background` / `foreground` come from OrielActivity's
  onStart/onStop (a new `NativeLib.onLifecycle` native, in the runtime
  synced into apps). The malloc purge on trim-memory is now a lifecycle
  subscriber, and also runs when the app goes to the background (it used
  to on TRIM_MEMORY_UI_HIDDEN). `oriel.android.onSystemEvent` still gets
  "trim-memory" and the Android-only events.
- The showcase frees its models through `unload_on` instead of
  `onSystemEvent` glue.
- `zig build check` for iOS without the SDK covers apps with C
  dependencies (`sql`, `llama`, `whisper`, `kokoro`): their C and C++
  type-check against Zig's own Darwin headers, and the Objective-C that
  needs framework headers (ggml's Metal backend, miniaudio's AVFoundation
  playback) and the frameworks themselves (Foundation, Metal, MetalKit)
  are left out. It failed before (missing libc, "unable to find
  framework"). With an SDK nothing changes.

## [Deprecated]($heading.id('deprecated-2'))

- `oriel.ios.onSystemEvent`: now a lifecycle subscriber under the old
  names ("background", "foreground", "memory-warning"); use
  `oriel.lifecycle`.
- `App.Config.on_session_end`: still called on Windows at session end,
  after `terminating`; subscribe to `oriel.lifecycle` instead.
- `oriel.android.preconnect`: not needed. The `io` Oriel hands the app
  resolves host names through bionic's getaddrinfo, so `std.http.Client`
  works on Android as it is (redirects included); remove the call. Oriel's
  own model downloads no longer use it.

## [Fixed]($heading.id('fixed-10'))

- Android projects generated before 0.9.9 now ship `zig-out/android-assets`
  (Oriel's runtime data and app extensions' `.android.assets`): their own
  `build.gradle.kts` never read it, so those files were left out of the APK
  silently. Oriel's managed `oriel-dependencies.gradle` adds the folder when
  the project doesn't.
- iOS 26 SDKs: Objective-C sources that import UIKit failed to compile
  (UIKit's headers include `<UIUtilities/UIDefines.h>`, from the SDK's
  `System/Library/SubFrameworks`, now on the framework path).
- iOS: `foreground` (and the old "foreground" system event) fired while the
  app launched; UIKit sends willEnterForeground then too. It now means
  "back from the background", as on Android.
- iOS: a build on a Mac without `-Dapple_sdk` or `$APPLE_SDK` found no SDK
  and couldn't link; it now asks `xcrun` for Xcode's (iPhoneOS or
  iPhoneSimulator, per target).

# [Oriel 0.9.16 · October 11, 2026]($heading.id('oriel-0-9-16-october-11-2026'))

Oriel 0.9.16 fixes what a code review against the NASA/JPL "Power of 10"
rules found: a way for `localStorage` to lose its data, use-after-free bugs
on window close and in Windows mDNS, pages that could hang their window,
replies reaching the wrong page, an unpinned download in the CLI, and
downgrades through `force`.

## [Fixed]($heading.id('fixed-11'))

- Native renderer: `localStorage` could lose everything. A key or value
  with a lone surrogate (valid in JavaScript) was saved as invalid UTF-8,
  the next launch couldn't read the file and started empty, and the next
  save overwrote it. Keys and values are stored as valid UTF-8 now, the
  read limit allows for JSON escaping, and a file that fails to load is
  moved aside (`localStorage.json.corrupt-<time>`) instead of overwritten.
- Native renderer: closing a window from a frame hook or a promise job
  freed the page while it was still running; the close now waits until the
  page is idle, on every backend.
- Native renderer: a script that never returns (`while (true) {}`, or a
  promise chain that keeps requeuing itself) no longer hangs the window for
  good. A call into JavaScript is stopped after 10 seconds, promise jobs
  run at most 10,000 per turn (the rest on the next), and the JavaScript
  heap is limited to 1 GiB.
- Native renderer on Linux and Windows: above 256 MB of decoded images the
  image cache was emptied and every image decoded again each frame; it now
  evicts the least recently used images.
- Windows mDNS: stopping a browse while lookups were finishing could use
  freed memory (lookups are reference-counted now) and race on the found
  services; lookups, names and addresses per browse are capped. Names,
  addresses and TXT keys with a NUL byte are refused.
- Events and replies reached only the page that should get them: on Windows
  and Android, an asynchronous reply or event could reach the next page
  after a navigation, and on every platform events went to whatever page a
  window showed, bridge or not.
- Windows: a failed request-filter registration, or an early error while
  serving an asset, let `https://app.localhost` requests reach the network;
  window creation fails, or the request gets a 500. macOS and iOS fail the
  request when a security header (CSP, nosniff) can't be set.
- macOS and iOS: `nativePath` always failed (the `F_GETPATH` result was
  misread).
- IPC: requests over 32 MiB are refused, the async job queue is capped
  (1024; extra calls answer `WorkerPoolBusy`), a message that can't be
  decoded gets an `InvalidRequest` reply instead of never settling, and
  window sizes from the page must be 1..32767.
- Updates: a manifest older than the running version is never installed by
  `force`; `oriel update --version` checks the tag's form and that the
  signed manifest is that version. Plain-HTTP manifest URLs only work in
  tests. A raw payload's size is capped, the replaced binary keeps its
  mode, and on Windows the single-instance lock is released only once the
  new process has started.
- `oriel` CLI: the iOS `xtool` download is pinned to 1.21.0 and its SHA-256
  checked (it was GitHub's unpinned latest release, run with your Apple
  ID). `oriel wrap` escapes the title, user agent and URL in the code and
  HTML it writes, and allows only the exact origin unless
  `--allow-subdomains` is given. Signing keys' temporary folders are
  created private (0700) from the start.
- Android builds: a version part that isn't a number or is 100 or more,
  and app names with `" $ \ & < > '`, are build errors instead of a wrong
  version code or a broken project.

# [Oriel 0.9.15 · October 11, 2026]($heading.id('oriel-0-9-15-october-11-2026'))

Oriel 0.9.15 makes Android mDNS discovery reliable on phones older than
Android 14, which could miss devices on the network.

## [Fixed]($heading.id('fixed-12'))

- Android mDNS browsing before Android 14: a resolve the system never
  answered blocked every service queued after it (one runs at a time), and
  a resolve that failed for any reason other than "busy" was never tried
  again, so the service stayed unlisted until browsing restarted. A
  resolve is now given up after 10 seconds, and failed ones are tried
  again after 1, 2, 4... seconds (up to 30) while the service is listed.
  Older phones could miss other devices on the network because of either.

# [Oriel 0.9.14 · October 10, 2026]($heading.id('oriel-0-9-14-october-10-2026'))

Oriel 0.9.14 fixes a crash: since 0.9.12, every app built with the native
renderer on Linux aborted as its window closed.

## [Fixed]($heading.id('fixed-13'))

- Native renderer on Linux: closing a window no longer aborts the app
  (SIGABRT). The text layouts kept per node (0.9.12) were freed before the
  page, whose nodes then removed themselves from the freed map; a release
  build caught it as a misaligned pointer. Every `-Dnative_ui` app on
  Linux crashed as its window closed.

# [Oriel 0.9.13 · October 10, 2026]($heading.id('oriel-0-9-13-october-10-2026'))

Oriel 0.9.13 lets VoiceOver reach the share picker in the native renderer
on macOS, and fixes what testing 0.9.12 on a Mac found in the system
monitor example: idle processes can be found and terminated, empty CPU
chips are hidden, and idle macOS network ports are left out.

## [Fixed]($heading.id('fixed-14'))

- Native renderer on macOS: a popover or sheet AppKit shows over the
  page (the share picker of `oriel.share.send`) is in the window's
  accessibility tree, so VoiceOver reaches it. The page view listed only
  the page's elements and left it out.
- System monitor example: the process filter reaches the sampler, so a
  match (by name, pid or, on macOS, user) is among the rows sent even
  when idle; filtering for `sleep` finds and can terminate it. Before,
  only the 256 busiest processes reached the page.
- System monitor example: the CPU header hides its frequency and
  temperature chips when there is no value (Apple Silicon), and macOS
  leaves out interfaces that never carried a byte (idle Thunderbolt and
  bridge ports); one that starts carrying traffic appears.
- System monitor example: the sampler tests print a sample taken half a
  second after the last, not a 0% CPU read 1.5 ms later.

# [Oriel 0.9.12 · October 10, 2026]($heading.id('oriel-0-9-12-october-10-2026'))

Oriel 0.9.12 keeps the native renderer's `localStorage` across runs, adds
macOS Services to share targets, and turns the system monitor example into
a btop-style monitor for Linux, Windows and macOS. Native renderer frames
on Linux are cheaper, and `JSON.parse` no longer depends on the locale.

## [Added]($heading.id('added-10'))

- Share targets on macOS: `.share_target` also gives the `.app` an entry
  in the Services menu, titled with the share label (else the app's name),
  shown without the user enabling it. It sends the selection's files of the
  declared types and, when the types take text, plain text and URLs; the app
  receives them as one share with source `service`, its files readable
  through `oriel.share.file`, and comes forward.

## [Changed]($heading.id('changed-7'))

- Native renderer: `localStorage` lasts beyond the run, as in a WebView.
  One store per app, shared by its windows, saved as JSON in
  `~/.config/<app id>/data/localStorage.json` (`$XDG_CONFIG_HOME` when
  set; `~/Library/Application Support/<app id>/data` on macOS,
  `%APPDATA%\<app id>\data` on Windows). Writes are batched (saved
  300 ms after a change, and when a window closes), and a 5 MB quota throws
  `QuotaExceededError` as browsers do. It was an in-memory map before, gone
  at exit. On Android and iOS it still lasts the run.
- The system-monitor example grew into a btop-style monitor: per-core CPU
  with frequencies and temperature, load, memory and swap, disks with I/O
  rates, network, sensors, system information, process user, threads and
  command line, a responsive layout, and Settings to show or hide each
  part (kept in `localStorage`). It runs on Linux, Windows and macOS (a
  sampler per OS; the page hides what an OS doesn't report), and a CI
  workflow tests each sampler and builds the packages (.deb, .rpm,
  .AppImage, setup.exe, .dmg). It costs less than btop and the terminal
  drawing it: a sampler at ~1.6 ms a sample and a virtual process table.

## [Fixed]($heading.id('fixed-15'))

- Native renderer: `JSON.parse` dropped the fraction of every number
  (`0.43` became `0`) under a locale whose decimal separator is a comma
  (`LC_NUMERIC=es_CL`, `de_DE`...): QuickJS read JSON numbers with
  `strtod`, which follows the locale GTK sets. It reads them with its own
  locale-independent `js_atod` now. Every command result reaching a page
  went through it.
- Windows builds with `-Dkokoro`: the build-time espeak-ng data compiler
  failed with `FileBusy` removing its staging directory, because the
  intonation compiler's log was left open.
- Native renderer on Linux: wheel scrolling no longer re-hovers a new
  element at every step. The compositor's motion after each step (the
  pointer still) hovered the row now under it, a restyle and a second
  redraw per step; `:hover` now waits until the scroll settles, as in
  browsers. The system-monitor example also drops its row hover
  transition, which kept the window redrawing every display frame while
  scrolling.
- Native renderer on Linux: cheaper frames. Each text keeps its Pango
  layout from frame to frame instead of being shaped again on every
  draw, and a box with rounded corners that clips its content clips it
  to a rectangle, with the rounded path only on what reaches into a
  corner (a path clip made Cairo composite everything under it through
  a mask). With the fix above, scrolling the system monitor's process
  table went from about 80% of a core to about 15%; a frame's paint
  takes about half as long.

# [Oriel 0.9.11 · October 9, 2026]($heading.id('oriel-0-9-11-october-9-2026'))

Oriel 0.9.11 adds `location.reload()` to the native renderer, makes live
tables much cheaper to update, and fixes the macOS and iOS builds of apps
with text to speech.

## [Added]($heading.id('added-11'))

- Native renderer: `location.reload()` starts the page again in its window,
  with a new JavaScript runtime and DOM. It runs on a turn of its own, after
  the calling script returns; answers to the old page's commands and its
  pending timers are dropped instead of reaching the new page.

## [Improved]($heading.id('improved-2'))

- Native renderer: text changes in place more often. A text cell whose box
  clips its text (`overflow: hidden`), a native button elsewhere on the
  page, and canvas programs that change in the same frame no longer force
  the page's rows to be rebuilt. The system monitor example went from
  1,000–2,400 rebuilt nodes (20–45 ms) per sample to a few hundred text
  updates (2–3 ms), and from about 6% to 1–2.5% of one core.
- The system monitor example reuses its table rows and writes text only
  when it changed.

## [Fixed]($heading.id('fixed-16'))

- The build-time espeak-ng data compiler builds on macOS and with Apple's
  SDK (it used stdio macros that translate-c turns into inline functions).
- iOS builds with `-Dkokoro`: espeak-ng gets its endian helpers on Apple
  targets, and miniaudio's playback compiles as Objective-C (AVFoundation).

# [Oriel 0.9.10 · October 9, 2026]($heading.id('oriel-0-9-10-october-9-2026'))

Oriel 0.9.10 makes `-Dnative_ui` apps on Linux free of WebKitGTK, and fixes
wide tables on the documentation site.

## [Improved]($heading.id('improved-3'))

- Linux apps built with `-Dnative_ui` no longer link or load WebKitGTK,
  JavaScriptCore or libsoup: every window is drawn by the native renderer.
  The system monitor example uses 14 MB less memory, loads 45 fewer shared
  libraries, and its stripped binary is 1.2 MB smaller.
- Their `.deb` and `.rpm` packages no longer depend on
  `libwebkitgtk-6.0-4` / `webkitgtk6.0`.
- The comparison with Vercel native now has measured numbers for the same
  system monitor app built with both frameworks.

## [Fixed]($heading.id('fixed-17'))

- Documentation tables wider than the page column scroll sideways instead of
  spilling under the table of contents; the module overview fits again.

## [Compatibility]($heading.id('compatibility'))

- On Linux with `-Dnative_ui`, `media_scheme.setRoot` returns
  `error.NoWebView`: there is no WebView to serve media to. The HTTP media
  server is unaffected. WebView builds are unchanged.

# [Oriel 0.9.9 · October 9, 2026]($heading.id('oriel-0-9-9-october-9-2026'))

Oriel 0.9.9 adds `oriel.tts`: offline text to speech that works out of the
box, with Kokoro-82M voices, verified downloads, bundled phoneme data and
streaming playback on the CPU or the GPU. It also brings mDNS to Windows,
drag and drop to the iOS native renderer, and Windows fixes.

## [Added]($heading.id('added-12'))

- `oriel.tts`: Kokoro-82M text to speech, the counterpart of `oriel.chat`
  and `oriel.dictation`. A catalog of models (`kokoro-82m-q8_0`, the
  default, and `kokoro-82m-f16`) and curated voices for English, Spanish,
  French, Portuguese, Italian, Japanese, Chinese and Hindi, downloaded from
  Hugging Face and checked against their SHA-256 (`download`, `delete`,
  `status`). `speak` reads Markdown as prose, guesses the language
  (`lang = "auto"`) and picks a voice for it, and streams playback in
  adaptive chunks: the first audio comes quickly and there are no gaps
  when synthesis is faster than real time. `stop` works from any thread;
  `warmUp` loads the model (and compiles GPU pipelines) ahead of the first
  utterance; `unloadIdle` frees it under memory pressure. Options cover
  model, voice, language, speed, Markdown, backend and threads; results
  report first-audio time, synthesis time, real-time factor and gaps.
  Events: "tts:download" and "tts:state".
- `audio_play.Stream`: streaming playback through a bounded ring, one device
  per utterance (`append`, `seal`, `finished`, `stop` from any thread,
  `playedSeconds`/`queuedSeconds`/`starvedSeconds`), next to the one-shot API.
- `model_download.fetchVerified`: downloads checked against a SHA-256; a
  mismatching file is deleted and `error.ChecksumMismatch` returned.
- espeak-ng's phoneme data is compiled at build time from the espeak-ng
  1.52.0 sources and shipped by `addApp` with every `.kokoro = true` app:
  next to the executable on Linux and Windows (and in their packages), in
  `Contents/Resources` on macOS, in the iOS `.app`, and in the Android APK's
  assets (extracted on first start). The `tts_languages` option picks the
  dictionaries (default `en,es,fr,pt,it,ja,cmn,hi`); `zig build
  espeak-data` writes the directory alone.
- Kokoro on the GPU with `-Dggml_cuda`, `-Dggml_vulkan` or `-Dggml_metal`,
  through ggml's backend registry, falling back to the CPU. Measured on an
  RTX 4070: first audio ~54 ms warm on CUDA, real-time factor ~0.02-0.03 on
  CUDA and Vulkan (Vulkan compiles its pipelines for 1-4 s on first use);
  8 threads of a Ryzen 7 7800X3D: ~0.36. Metal compiles in but hasn't been
  measured.
- Windows `oriel.network.mdns` backend on dnsapi's `DnsServiceRegister`,
  `DnsServiceBrowse` and `DnsServiceResolve` (Windows 10 1809+). TXT values
  must be UTF-8 text there.
- iOS native renderer: drag and drop into the page through
  `UIDropInteraction`: files (by data type), Photos images, text, and links
  (`text/uri-list` and `text/plain`), matching AppKit's.
- Showcase: a Speak tab (voices, models, speed, CPU/GPU, Markdown, live
  state, a result card with first audio, real-time factor and gaps), Read
  aloud on chat replies and notes, a warm-up on the tab's first visit, and
  the `--say` and `--tts-download` headless flags.

## [Improved]($heading.id('improved-4'))

- Phones run Kokoro on their fast cores only and shorten the first chunk to
  the measured speed. Low-end phones still synthesize slower than real
  time: a Snapdragon 732G (2 threads) reaches a real-time factor of ~2.75,
  with first audio after ~4 s and pauses between chunks.
- `oriel dev` rebuilds keep a Zig `--fork=<path>` option.
- Windows tray icons are decoded with zigimg instead of WIC, so creating and
  destroying trays no longer grows the process's kernel handles.

## [Fixed]($heading.id('fixed-18'))

- Windows: closing a window or quitting while a native-renderer context menu
  or the tray menu is open no longer hangs; a native page that closes its
  own window from a control's handler is closed once that handler returns.
- Native-renderer sliders with huge ranges reach their min and max on
  Windows, and the page-side value no longer becomes `Infinity`.
- Windows `dialog.saveToFolder` refuses names Win32 programs can't open
  (device names such as `CON` or `nul.txt`, a trailing dot or space,
  `<>:"|?*`) with `error.InvalidName`.
- Android: `audio_play` builds with NDK 28 (nullability qualifiers in its
  headers).
- Windows: Kokoro links espeak-ng statically (`LIBESPEAK_NG_EXPORT`), so
  its API is no longer declared as imported from a DLL.

## [Compatibility]($heading.id('compatibility-2'))

- `KOKORO_ESPEAK_DATA_PATH` is now optional: apps no longer supply
  espeak-ng data. Set it only to use other data than the bundled copy.
- Android projects generated before this release need one line in
  `app/build.gradle.kts`, inside `android { }`:
  `sourceSets["main"].assets.srcDirs("../../zig-out/android-assets")`
  (the path from the app module to `zig-out`). The Kotlin runtime is
  updated by the build.
- Apps built with `.kokoro = true` now ship espeak-ng's data
  (GPL-3.0-or-later, about 2.9 MB with the default languages); see `NOTICE`.

# [Oriel 0.9.8 · October 9, 2026]($heading.id('oriel-0-9-8-october-9-2026'))

Oriel 0.9.8 is a memory-safety release. It fixes use-after-free, out-of-bounds,
overflow and leak defects across the native UI, platform layers, modules and
CLI tooling.

## [Fixed]($heading.id('fixed-19'))

- Update manifests: base64 signatures, public keys and seeds must decode to
  exactly their expected size, closing a stack overflow reachable from an
  untrusted manifest before signature verification.
- Native DOM: mutation observer hooks no longer run page code in the middle of
  a store operation, so observers can no longer corrupt or free nodes still in
  use. `host.canvas` re-reads its buffer after page code can run, wrappers are
  re-validated after allocation, `:nth-child` arithmetic no longer overflows,
  and serialization and deep cloning no longer recurse per tree level.
- Native UI: repeating gradients with many stops no longer write past a stack
  buffer, focus and blur no longer run page code while fields are being
  synchronized (GTK, UIKit), page geometry and slider values are clamped before
  integer conversion, and uninitialized text layout offsets, error-path leaks,
  stale event pointers and unreleased accessibility elements are fixed.
- Win32: clipboard paste is bounded by the clipboard allocation, controls are
  no longer destroyed under an open context menu, and GDI, font-cache and event
  handle leaks are fixed.
- Android: native-page commands are queued instead of running inside the
  page's own JavaScript, so a command that closes its window can no longer free
  the running engine. Dropped-file descriptors and window sizes are handled
  safely.
- Linux: a rejected window URL no longer leaks a window with a dangling close
  handler, an empty second-instance command line no longer crashes the primary
  instance, and windows, queued replies and timers are released at shutdown.
- macOS and iOS: pending WebKit replies are answered before release at
  shutdown, and single-instance and scene teardown no longer reuse closed
  descriptors or freed windows. Windows `showWindow` no longer reads window
  state off the main thread.
- Modules and plugins: fixes for the macOS file watcher, the store keeping
  borrowed JSON values, Linux menu and tray leaks, tray D-Bus depth handling,
  clipboard image decoding, X11 shortcut re-entry, Windows share events and
  the Wayland keymap.
- AI and audio: empty SQL statements, WAV sample rates, prompt and download
  sizes, grammar sampling and empty prompts are validated; GPU loaders clear
  their function pointers when a library fails to load.
- CLI and tooling: `oriel wrap` no longer returns a name from freed memory,
  Android manifest sync no longer reads stale keys, confirmation prompts accept
  Enter, long deep-link schemes work, and network responses are size-bounded.

## [Compatibility]($heading.id('compatibility-3'))

- Unpadded or otherwise mis-sized base64 signatures and keys in update
  manifests are rejected with `error.InvalidPadding`.
- Native pages: layout stops at 512 levels of nesting, gradients use at most
  256 stops, and selectors are limited to 16 nested `:not`/`:is`/`:has` and 32
  compound parts (longer ones throw `SyntaxError`).
- Window URLs containing control characters are rejected when the window is
  opened. `App.getWindow` and `App.getWindowByHandle` are main-thread only.
- New errors: `error.SqliteEmptyStatement` (`oriel.sql`),
  `error.PromptTooLong`, `error.EmptyPrompt` and `error.SampleFailed` (chat),
  `error.DownloadTooLarge` (model downloads). WAV input below 4000 Hz is
  rejected.

# [Oriel 0.9.7 · October 8, 2026]($heading.id('oriel-0-9-7-october-8-2026'))

Oriel 0.9.7 adds opt-in offline text-to-speech with Kokoro-82M and
built-in PCM playback.

## [Added]($heading.id('added-13'))

- `oriel.kokoro`: typed C-ABI bindings for model initialization, voice loading,
  language selection, synthesis, error reporting and PCM cleanup.
- `oriel.audio_play`: mono float32 playback through miniaudio, with progress,
  natural-completion callbacks, stop/shutdown, and `finished()` polling.
- Enable TTS with `.kokoro = true` alongside `.llama = true` or `.whisper = true`.
  Kokoro shares Oriel's GGML build and adds espeak-ng phonemization and Highway
  SIMD. Apps supply model/voice GGUF files and compatible espeak-ng runtime data.
- `AppOptions.include_paths` forwards app-owned C include directories to desktop,
  iOS, Android and TypeScript-generation root modules.
- Repository and website guides for offline speech synthesis, asset setup,
  playback ownership, callback behavior and dependency notices.

## [Fixed]($heading.id('fixed-20'))

- Playback teardown detaches sample state before joining the device thread,
  preventing stop/start deadlocks with audio callbacks.
- Lifecycle operations are serialized; stop and finished are safe before
  initialization, and stopped/completed audio buffers are filled with silence.
- Playback implementation and Zig declarations use the same vendored header,
  including whisper-only TTS builds and coexistence with llama's mtmd audio code.
- Optional TTS dependencies are downloaded lazily, keeping default builds lean.
- Invalid synthesis output is rejected before converting C sample counts/rates.
- macOS TTS builds use espeak-ng's upstream endian compatibility header.

## [Validation and dependencies]($heading.id('validation-and-dependencies'))

- TTS-enabled tests exercise ABI defaults, PCM buffer completion, and repeated
  start/stop against miniaudio's silent null backend. Linux CPU synthesis is
  verified with the local Kokoro Q8_0 model and English voice pack.
- Release CI enables TTS tests on Linux and macOS in addition to the existing
  framework, CLI, Windows and iOS checks. GPU synthesis and physical audio-device
  behavior are not claimed as verified across every platform.
- The optional TTS stack includes espeak-ng (GPL-3.0-or-later). Oriel's framework
  remains MIT; upstream component license texts are included in `NOTICE` and
  `licenses/tts`. Model assets are supplied separately by apps.

# [Oriel 0.9.5 · October 8, 2026]($heading.id('oriel-0-9-5-october-8-2026'))

Oriel 0.9.5 fixes the validated concurrency, memory-lifetime and security
issues from a security and memory-safety review, and documents which reported
findings were not supported by the current code.

## [Fixed]($heading.id('fixed-21'))

- File-handle tables synchronize access through reads and descriptor
  duplication, preventing release from recycling a descriptor in use.
- Worker-pool shutdown withdraws the pool, waits for borrowed references,
  drains accepted jobs, and rejects later submissions without leaking them.
- Async command errors retain their own text until the UI consumes the reply.
- Lazy window creation snapshots registered options before releasing the lock.
- Cross-window `emitTo` now checks the same modification permission as other
  window mutations. Smoke and Showcase explicitly opt into cross-window events.
- Isolation frames validate message origins and send replies only to explicit
  local origins; the Windows/Android bridge skips subframes.
- iOS Inbox cleanup only removes canonical files directly inside the app's own
  Inbox, using directory-relative deletion.
- Gzip update expansion is capped at 512 MiB in memory, on disk, and during
  macOS bundle verification/extraction. Expiring manifests reject invalid clocks.
- Windows media streams serialize Read and Seek and advance by actual bytes read.
- The local media server validates loopback Host headers on every route and
  scopes its ping response's CORS header to the configured app origin.
- Zig model downloads require HTTPS on initial URLs and redirects.
- Share-read and dev-server allocation failures release intermediate buffers;
  internal window and received-file cleanup consistently follows its allocator.

## [Documentation and validation]($heading.id('documentation-and-validation'))

- Added the website's **Wrap a web app** guide and navigation links.
- Validated every finding in the security and memory-safety review, correcting ownership, tar extraction,
  token exposure, command-line reader, and optional expiration claims.
- Added regression tests for concurrency, error lifetimes, window permissions,
  path guards, Host validation, HTTPS URLs, gzip limits, and isolation messages.

## [Compatibility]($heading.id('compatibility-4'))

- Cross-window events require
  `security.window_api.allow_modify_other_windows = true`.
- `App.getWorkerPool()` borrows must be paired with `App.releaseWorkerPool()`;
  `ThreadPool.post()` returns whether it accepted ownership of a task.
- Publishers must keep expanded gzip update payloads within the 512 MiB limit.

# [Oriel 0.9.4 · October 8, 2026]($heading.id('oriel-0-9-4-october-8-2026'))

Oriel 0.9.4 adds desktop wrappers for web apps, app-owned Android extensions,
and searchable documentation.

## [Added]($heading.id('added-14'))

- `oriel wrap <url>` (alias `oriel pake`) scaffolds a desktop app for a website,
  with tray controls, close-to-tray behavior, automatic favicon fetching,
  optional Linux user-agent overrides, and AppImage packaging.
- App-owned Android extensions can handle Activity and WebView lifecycles,
  permissions, activity results, and file selection without editing generated
  runtime sources. Configure extension classes, Maven SDK dependencies, and
  repositories in the app's build configuration.
- Android app manifests can declare vendor native libraries with
  `uses-native-library` entries.
- Pagefind search on the documentation site, with keyboard navigation and
  indexed article content.

## [Improved]($heading.id('improved-5'))

- Android templates now use Android Gradle Plugin 8.13.2 and Kotlin 2.3.21,
  with the typed Kotlin JVM target configuration.
- Native-renderer documentation describes renderer capabilities independently
  of individual release versions.

## [Fixed]($heading.id('fixed-22'))

- Linux disables WebKitGTK DMABUF rendering when the proprietary NVIDIA driver
  is detected, avoiding compositor-related high CPU usage. An explicit
  `WEBKIT_DISABLE_DMABUF_RENDERER` setting remains respected.

# [Oriel 0.9.3 · October 5, 2026]($heading.id('oriel-0-9-3-october-5-2026'))

- Android templates and examples now compile and target API 36, meeting the
  current Google Play target requirement. Updated AGP to 8.9.3 and generated
  Gradle wrappers to 8.14.3; minimum Gradle is 8.11.1. Android 10 remains supported.

# [Oriel 0.9.2 · October 5, 2026]($heading.id('oriel-0-9-2-october-5-2026'))

Oriel 0.9.2 lets a page find out where a dropped file lives now, so a handler
can send it on to the system.

## [Added]($heading.id('added-15'))

- `oriel.drop.path(file)` for native-renderer pages: where a dropped file lives
  now, for a handler that forwards it (a transfer engine opens the path). The
  `File` a drop delivers carries an opaque `handle`; the answer is a path
  string, or `null` when the file was deleted or changed since the drop, the
  handle is unknown, or the platform can't resolve paths. The Linux and Windows
  native bridges answer it after the command policy check; a WebView page has
  no handles and no `drop:path`. Electron's `webUtils.getPathForFile` is the
  precedent. See `docs/drag-and-drop-design.md`.

# [Oriel 0.9.1 · October 5, 2026]($heading.id('oriel-0-9-1-october-5-2026'))

Oriel 0.9.1 adds system sharing, persistent folder access and service discovery,
and improves native controls, accessibility and platform integration.

## [Added]($heading.id('added-16'))

- `oriel.network.mdns` service registration and browsing, with Android's
  NsdManager backend and the `oriel_mdns_*` C ABI for native libraries.
- System sharing APIs for sending content, receiving shared files and reading
  received files from pages, with platform capability reporting.
- Folder picking with lasting write access, including Android's Storage Access
  Framework and iOS folder access.
- `oriel.system.deviceName()` to read the name the user gave their device.
- Bluetooth and local-network permissions, per-platform permission extras,
  and support for app-owned Android Kotlin/Java sources and R8 rules.
- Optional Windows MSIX packaging and packaged Share Target support.
- Native push buttons, checkboxes and radio controls across supported backends;
  Apple accessibility trees for VoiceOver and macOS assistive technology.
- Native renderer forced-colors support, CSS system colors and contrast media
  queries, including Windows high-contrast settings.
- Windows native-renderer drag and drop, context menus and pointer handling.

## [Improved]($heading.id('improved-6'))

- Native controls follow dark mode and system accent changes, with improved
  keyboard behavior, read-only fields and accessible names.
- Windows 11 field styling, typography and overlay scrollbars.
- Native text layout, inline geometry, baselines, lists, fieldsets and borders
  across Linux, Windows, macOS, Android and iOS.
- Events received before page listeners are ready are queued for delivery.

## [Fixed]($heading.id('fixed-23'))

- Android mDNS resolution no longer crashes while encoding services with
  multiple TXT attributes. Attributes are iterated directly rather than copied
  through Android ArrayMap's unsupported entry-set `toArray()` method.
- Windows packages fail clearly when WebView2Loader.dll is missing.
- Windows sharing preserves existing OpenWithProgids registrations.
- Native controls avoid spurious radio changes, clipped select controls and
  accidental button taps while scrolling on iOS.

# [Oriel 0.9.0 · October 4, 2026]($heading.id('oriel-0-9-0-october-4-2026'))

Oriel 0.9.0 lets apps react to notification clicks and buttons, brings
drag and drop to the native renderer, and closes desktop input gaps for
ChromeOS and desktop Android. Windows support for these lands in 0.9.1. See [Notifications]($link.page('docs/modules')) for the notification API.

## [Added]($heading.id('added-17'))

- Notification click actions: `oriel.notification.notify` takes `.actions`
  (buttons), and clicks on a notification or one of its buttons reach
  `oriel.notification.onAction(handler)` in Zig and the page's
  `notification:action` event as `{ id, action }` (`action` is null when the
  notification itself was clicked).
  - Linux: GNotification default action and buttons.
  - macOS (`.app` bundles) and iOS: `UNNotificationCategory` buttons and the
    notification center delegate.
  - Android: a tap opens the app and reports the click, also when it
    launches the app; buttons (at most 3) report theirs without opening the
    app and dismiss the notification.
  - Windows: a click on the balloon is reported; balloons have no buttons.
- The showcase's Notify button sends a notification with Like and Later
  buttons and shows which one was clicked.
- Drag and drop into native-renderer pages (`-Dnative_ui`) on Linux,
  Android and macOS: `DragEvent`, `DataTransfer`, `Blob`, `File`,
  `FileReader` and `FileList`. Dropped files are opened read-only when they
  are dropped and read asynchronously; the page never sees their paths. See
  `docs/drag-and-drop-design.md`.
- The page's `<meta name="theme-color">` is reported to its window in both
  renderers; on ChromeOS and desktop Android it colours the window's caption.
- Native renderer on Android: a mouse right-click opens the page's context
  menu, and the pointer shows a hand over clickable elements (links amid text
  included) and an I-beam over text fields.

## [Improved]($heading.id('improved-7'))

- Native renderer mouse events report the changed `button` (2 for the
  secondary, 1 for the middle button), fire `auxclick` after a non-primary
  release, and give a mouse's `contextmenu` button 2. macOS sends right,
  middle and other buttons as WKWebView does.
- Links and other clickable elements inside a line of text take their own
  clicks in the native renderer.
- Native renderer border widths snap to device pixels as each platform's
  browser does; key scrolling glides like WKWebView's; `scrollWidth` matches
  WebKit's horizontal range.
- Android: `white-space: pre` text keeps its line breaks.

## [Security]($heading.id('security'))

- A file dropped on a text field no longer inserts its absolute path: native
  fields leave drops to the page, and in WebView mode the bridge cancels
  WebKit's default for a file dropped on an unhandled field.

# [Oriel 0.8.0 · October 4, 2026]($heading.id('oriel-0-8-0-october-4-2026'))

Oriel 0.8.0 introduces the optional, experimental native UI renderer alongside
the default system WebView. The same app HTML, CSS and JavaScript run in
QuickJS and draw through Oriel's native platform backends.

## [Added]($heading.id('added-18'))

- Build with `-Dnative_ui` on Linux, Windows, macOS, Android and iOS.
- `oriel android dev/build` and `oriel ios dev/build` forward Zig `-D` options.
  For example: `oriel android build --abi arm64 --apk -Dnative_ui
  -Doptimize=ReleaseFast`.
- Android projects for the Breakout, Canvas Demo and render-bench examples.
- Power sampling instructions and support for Android battery readings and
  Linux battery or RAPL counters.

## [Improved]($heading.id('improved-8'))

- Android suspends native animation frames while a window is hidden and resumes
  them when it becomes visible.
- Android text metrics, controls, scrolling, input and slider endpoints more
  closely match the platform WebView.
- Native canvas recording and transform updates do less per-frame work.
- Inline text, SVG styling, line layout, gradients and DOM cleanup have been
  improved across platform backends.
- Breakout bounds long-running ball speed, and its Zig mode applies the ball
  count selected in Settings.

## [Performance notes]($heading.id('performance-notes'))

The Android x86_64 emulator completed the render bench at about 60 fps for
animation and canvas workloads. This validates that build; it is not an
Android before/after comparison and does not measure battery savings. The
desktop QuickJS dispatch changes reduced isolated callback overhead, while
the full desktop comparison showed no clear overall speedup. See the
[benchmark results](https://github.com/highercomve/Oriel/tree/main/examples/render-bench/results).

The native renderer remains experimental and supports a subset of browser
HTML, CSS and APIs. The default WebView build remains available.
